.\" Manual page for hello(1).
.\"
.\" Rendered with:  man ./hello.1
.\" Checked with:   mandoc -Tlint hello.1
.\"
.TH HELLO 1 "2026-08-22" "hello 1.2.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
hello \- print a greeting to standard output
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hello
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hello
writes the string
.RB \(lq "Hello, World!" \(rq
followed by a newline to standard output, and exits.
.PP
That is the entire behaviour.
.B hello
accepts no options, reads no input, consults no configuration, opens no
files, performs no network activity, and retains no state between
invocations. Arguments supplied on the command line are ignored.
.PP
The program's purpose is diagnostic. Its output is not the product; the
product is the demonstration that a compiler, assembler, linker, loader,
C runtime, kernel, terminal driver, and display pipeline are all
functioning together on the host. A program that computes something can
fail in two indistinguishable ways \(em the apparatus is broken, or the
logic is wrong.
.B hello
has no logic to get wrong, which makes it the smallest question that
still requires the entire system to answer.
.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
.PP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version ", and every other argument are accepted and"
ignored; the program prints its greeting regardless. This is deliberate.
See
.BR "README section 7" .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. Emitted unconditionally.
.PP
The exit status space is eight bits wide.
.B hello
uses one value of the 256 available. Note that
.B 0
does not indicate that the greeting was received, or was good; it
indicates only that nothing went wrong. See
.B CAVEATS
below.
.
.SH OUTPUT
Exactly fourteen bytes on file descriptor 1:
.PP
.RS
.nf
0000000   H  e  l  l  o  ,     W  o  r  l  d  !  \en
0000016
.fi
.RE
.PP
Nothing is written to standard error.
.PP
Standard output is line buffered when attached to a terminal and fully
buffered otherwise. The trailing newline triggers the flush in the
former case; in the latter the buffer is flushed at exit.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Run it:
.B hello
.TP
Verify the exit status:
.B hello; echo $?
.TP
Inspect the exact bytes emitted:
.B hello | od \-c
.TP
Confirm the byte count:
.B hello | wc \-c
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B hello
reads no environment variables.
.PP
Several nonetheless affect what is observed, none through any action of
the program:
.TP
.B TERM
Determines how the terminal renders the bytes. Not read by
.BR hello .
.TP
.BR LANG ", " LC_ALL
Have no effect. The output is pure 7-bit ASCII and is not localized.
.TP
.B LD_PRELOAD
May replace
.BR printf (3)
entirely, at which point no guarantee in this page holds.
.
.SH FILES
None.
.B hello
neither reads nor writes any file. It has no configuration file, no
state directory, no cache, and no log.
.
.SH CAVEATS
The return value of
.BR printf (3)
is not checked. If the write fails \(em the disk is full, the pipe is
closed, the terminal has gone away \(em
.B hello
does not detect it and still exits
.BR 0 ,
reporting success for something that did not occur.
.PP
This is reproducible:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hello >&\-
.fi
.RE
.PP
The write fails, nothing is printed, and the exit status is
.BR 0 .
This is a genuine defect, present in essentially every published version
of this program including the original. It is documented rather than
fixed; see
.B "README section 12.2"
for the reasoning.
.
.SH CONFORMING TO
.B hello
requires a hosted C implementation. It builds clean under every C
standard from C89 through C23, and as C++98 through C++20, with
.B \-Wall \-Wextra \-pedantic \-Werror
and no diagnostics.
.PP
It does not build on a freestanding implementation, which has no
.BR stdio (3)
and no runtime to call
.BR main ().
.
.SH BUGS
See
.B "README section 12"
for the complete list of known limitations, of which the
.BR printf (3)
return value above is the only functional one.
.PP
Report bugs via the repository's issue tracker. Note that the
overwhelming majority of reports are toolchain or environment faults
rather than defects in six lines of source;
.B "INSTRUCTIONS.md Part 15"
covers fifteen common failures and their causes.
.
.SH HISTORY
The phrase first appears in Brian Kernighan's
.I "A Tutorial Introduction to the Language B"
(Bell Laboratories, 1972), and was fixed permanently in the culture by
the first page of Kernighan and Ritchie,
.IR "The C Programming Language" ,
Prentice-Hall, 1978.
.PP
The original was lowercase and carried no exclamation point:
.IR "hello, world" .
The capitals and the exclamation mark are a later redaction.
.PP
Kernighan has said the phrase came to him from a cartoon of a newly
hatched chick. The complete history of the sentence, of the language it
is written in, and of the language it is written with, is in the
commentary of
.IR hello.c ,
which runs to 2,712 lines.
.
